\DOC SIMPLE_ORTHOGONAL_COMPONENTS_TAC

\TYPE {SIMPLE_ORTHOGONAL_COMPONENTS_TAC : tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Proves goals asserting that two state components are orthogonal, closing them
outright or reducing them to an unfolded orthogonality claim.

\DESCRIBE
Takes no arguments. Applied to an assumption-free goal
{orthogonal_components t u}, it usually solves it outright:
{
   ?- orthogonal_components t u
   ==========================  SIMPLE_ORTHOGONAL_COMPONENTS_TAC
}
\noindent This covers pairings of registers, vector-register subfields, flags,
memory byte/bytelist components and {rvalue} terms. In some cases it instead
leaves one goal in which the component names have been expanded into their
underlying definitional form:
{
   ?- orthogonal_components t u
   ==========================  SIMPLE_ORTHOGONAL_COMPONENTS_TAC
   ?- orthogonal_components t' u'
}
\noindent where {t'} and {u'} are the expanded components.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only goals of this orthogonality shape with no
assumptions.

\ENDDOC
